    "is_reverted": false,
    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe root cause is the use of inline \"if-then-else\" (conditional expression) syntax in Alloy 6, which requires the condition to be a Boolean formula, but here, the construct some et: EmergencyTrigger | ... => ... else ... is being used directly as the right-hand side of an assignment, which is not valid syntax. Specifically, in Alloy 6, \"=>\" is implication, not \"if-then-else,\" and the use of \"else\" after \"=>\" is not valid Alloy. The entire construct at line 210-212 is thus not recognized as a formula or valid value expression. Prior failed fixes tried to patch this with extra parentheses or by reworking the comprehension/let, but never addressed the need to use Alloy's ternary conditional expression (if c then x else y) or to flatten the logic using set comprehensions and quantifiers.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nReplace the invalid \"implication ... else ...\" construct with Alloy's proper ternary conditional expression, or rephrase the assignment using a set comprehension that directly yields the intended set.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Navigate to pred R1R2R3, specifically the block:\n  ```\n  s.emergencyAdmins = (\n      some et: EmergencyTrigger | et.session = s.session && TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt]) \n          => {et: EmergencyTrigger | et.session = s.session && TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt])}.admins\n          else none\n  )\n  ```\n- Step 2: Replace this assignment with the following Alloy 6-compliant expression:\n  ```\n  s.emergencyAdmins = \n      { a: Administrator | \n          some et: EmergencyTrigger | \n              a in et.admins && \n              et.session = s.session && \n              TO/lte[et.at, s.t] && \n              (no et.endedAt or TO/lt[s.t, et.endedAt])\n      }\n  ```\n  This directly defines s.emergencyAdmins as the set of Administrators who are admins of some EmergencyTrigger meeting the session, start, and end conditions.\n- Step 3: Remove the invalid \"=> ... else ...\" syntax entirely; do not attempt to use Alloy's \"if ... then ... else ...\" unless you use the correct ternary syntax (which would also be valid as an alternative, but the above is the simplest set comprehension).\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis approach eliminates the misused implication/else construct and instead uses a set comprehension to define s.emergencyAdmins, which is valid Alloy 6 syntax and avoids the previously failed attempts at patching with parentheses, let, or implication constructs.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen needing to assign a set conditionally based on the existence of certain objects, use set comprehensions in Alloy; do not attempt to use \"=> ... else ...\" for value assignment—Alloy does not support that syntax.",
