    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The user has clarified the requirement by specifying Emergency mode must end after 5 time steps (timeout) or be ended early by the triggering administrators, and that the session must start in Normal mode, enter Emergency, and eventually return to Normal. This enables targeted model repair and requirement update.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable (pending required model repairs)\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertR2_EventualReturnToNormal fails as expected under prior model)\n- Instances: N/A (no meaningful instances due to missing Emergency mode ending)\n- Vacuity: Possible (requirements predicates UNSAT, leading to vacuity)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: The clarified requirement enables model repair, but the current model does not yet implement the 5-step timeout termination for Emergency mode. Assertion and predicate failures will persist until the model is updated accordingly. Convergence is blocked pending these changes.\n\n**CRITICAL CONVERGENCE CRITERIA:**\nRecommend FALSE because:\n- Predicates remain UNSAT due to missing Emergency mode timeout\n- Requirement ambiguity is now resolved, but model must be updated and retested\n- No meaningful satisfying instances yet (until model is repaired)\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n- Update the model to enforce Emergency mode termination after 5 time steps or allow early termination by the triggering administrators:\n    1. Introduce a fact or constraint to enforce that for any EmergencyTrigger et, Emergency mode ends either when explicitly ended by the triggering admins or automatically after 5 time steps (counting time steps since et.at).\n        - Example Alloy fact:\n            ```\n            fact EmergencyTimeoutOrEarlyEnd {\n               all et: EmergencyTrigger | \n                 some s: State | \n                   (s.session = et.session && \n                    ((s.mode = NormalMode && #(TO/nexts[et.at] & upto[s.t, s.t+4]) = 5) ||\n                     (s.mode = NormalMode && endedEarlyByAdmins[et, s])))\n            }\n            ```\n            (Adjust as needed for your model's time/step representation and admin-ending logic.)\n    2. Ensure the initial state for each session is Normal mode.\n    3. Add/adjust assertions and run commands to test the required scenario: start in Normal, transition to Emergency, and return to Normal after 5 steps or earlier if ended by admins.\n    4. Remove or update any facts or constraints that previously blocked Emergency mode ending due to missing termination mechanism.\n    5. After implementing the above, rerun all assertions and check for satisfiability and meaningful instance generation.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Add a fact such as: \"all et: EmergencyTrigger | some s: State | s.session = et.session && s.mode = NormalMode && (et.endedAt = s.t or #TO/nexts[et.at] >= N)\" for some N, to force Emergency mode to end after N steps.\n2. Relax the requirement that only the same two admins can end Emergency mode, to test whether overconstraint is the blocking factor.\n3. Temporarily treat endedAt as always assigned after a fixed abstract duration, to see if any instance satisfies liveness.\n4. Isolate R2 in a run command with a minimal scenario (single session, two admins, one EmergencyTrigger) and trace the possible mode transitions.\n5. Remove or abstract the endedAt field entirely, and instead introduce a fact that Emergency mode cannot persist indefinitely, to check if this allows satisfaction and liveness.\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: requirement gap (now resolved)\n- Issue: The mechanism for Emergency mode termination was previously ambiguous; now clarified to be a 5-step timeout or early termination by the triggering admins.\n- Rationale: The original model could not prove liveness or scenario termination due to lack of a specified end condition for Emergency mode.\n- Action: No further requirement update needed; proceed to implement the clarified mechanism in the model.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: Emergency mode duration and termination are now concrete (5 time steps timeout or early admin end).\n- Category: keep\n- Rationale: User has explicitly specified the mechanism, so the assumption is now justified and actionable.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R2: Emergency Mode Duration\n- Issue Type: ambiguity (now resolved)\n- Evidence: User has clarified the requirement; Emergency mode must end after 5 time steps or by early admin action.\n- Recommended Update: Update R2 to read: \"Emergency mode for a session must end after 5 time steps (timeout) unless ended earlier by the two triggering administrators.\"\n- Implication: The model can now enforce liveness and termination, and the property \"Emergency mode does not persist indefinitely\" becomes directly verifiable.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ&A-1: confirmed - User has specified the Emergency mode termination mechanism as required (5-step timeout or early admin end)\nQ&A-2: confirmed - Model should enforce both timeout and early termination as per user instruction\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Implement the 5-step timeout or early admin termination mechanism for Emergency mode as per clarified requirement.\n- Update initial state to Normal mode, enforce required scenario transitions, and rerun all verification.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Add/adjust assertions and run commands to ensure scenario coverage and meaningful instance generation.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- None at this time.\n```",
