    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe root cause of the syntax error is not simply the conjunction of quantifiers or block structuring, but that Alloy 6 expects a single Boolean expression as the body of a function, and joining multiple quantified formulas at the top level (with \"&&\", \"and\", or even as sequential statements in a function) is not permitted. All prior attempts, including using predicates, functions, blocks, or assertion blocks, still relied on top-level conjunction of quantifiers—which Alloy's parser rejects in these contexts. Instead, the correct approach is to combine the requirements into a single quantified formula by expressing the property as a universal quantification over State with an embedded conditional, using predicates or let-bindings as needed to decompose the logic, rather than as a conjunction of two separate quantifiers.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nFlatten the logic into a single quantified formula by quantifying over State and using let-bindings (or an embedded iff) to express both conditions in a single Boolean formula.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: Remove the function EventualReturnToNormal[et: EmergencyTrigger] entirely.\n- Step 2: In the assertion assertR2_EventualReturnToNormal, replace the call to EventualReturnToNormal[et] with the following single formula (written in Alloy 6 syntax):\n    all et: EmergencyTrigger | \n      (some s: State | s.session = et.session and TO/lte[s.t, et.endedAt] and s.mode = NormalMode)\n      and \n      (all s: State | (s.session = et.session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]) implies s.mode = NormalMode)\n- Step 3: If Alloy 6 still rejects this, flatten further by using a let-binding for the set of States after endedAt, e.g.,\n    all et: EmergencyTrigger | \n      let afterStates = {s: State | s.session = et.session and TO/gte[s.t, et.endedAt]} |\n        (some s: afterStates | s.mode = NormalMode)\n        and\n        (all s: afterStates | s.mode = NormalMode)\n- Step 4: Ensure no explicit \"and\"/\"&&\" is used at the top level of a function or predicate body, and that all logic is embedded within a single quantified formula (possibly with let-bindings).\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix is Alloy 6 compliant because it expresses the full property as a single quantified formula, avoiding any top-level conjunction of quantifiers or statements, and is distinct from all previous attempts that relied on combining separate quantifiers via \"and\" or blocks.\n\n[LESSON]:\nWhen Alloy 6 rejects top-level conjunction of quantifiers or Boolean statements, rewrite the logic as a single quantified formula (possibly using let-bindings) to avoid explicit conjunctions at the top level.\n```",
