r to the first time step of Normal mode. No state in Normal mode may have any emergency privileges assigned or persisted.\n- Rerun all verification predicates and assertions after model changes.\n- Remove or refactor any model code that previously enforced cross-mode persistence bans on delegations originating in Normal mode, ensuring only Emergency-mode delegations are subject to strict revocation at transition.\n- **MANDATORY**: Retest DelegationCoverage, DelegationCoverageEmergency, DelegationCoverageNormal, and assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs with the updated model.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\nStep 1: Relax the DelegationCoverage predicate to split the requirement into two separate runs: one asserting existence of a delegation in Emergency mode, one in Normal mode (already done—DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverageNormal).\nStep 2: Run DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverageNormal separately to verify if either is independently satisfiable.\nStep 3: Analyze the minimal scenario where delegations are created independently in each mode, ensuring no cross-mode persistence.\nStep 4: Temporarily remove or weaken the EmergencyDelegationAndPrivilegeRevocation fact to test if the only blocker is forced revocation at Emergency/Normal transition.\nStep 5: Add a diagnostic run that allows a delegation to be created immediately after Emergency ends in Normal mode, to confirm that distinct delegations per mode are possible.\nStep 6: If above tests pass, confirm with requirements owner whether it is sufficient for coverage to witness two distinct delegations (one per mode), or if a more complex scenario is required.\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: requirement ambiguity (now addressed)\n- Issue: Prior ambiguity in R8.1 as to whether DelegationCoverage is a strict system invariant or verification-only property.\n- Rationale: Model and verification conflicts are now resolved by user clarification.\n- Action: No further action required; update model to match clarified intent.\n\n- Issue type: requirement ambiguity (now addressed)\n- Issue: Unclear rules for persistence of delegations across Emergency/Normal transitions (R4.2).\n- Rationale: User has clarified precise rules for Emergency and Normal mode delegations.\n- Action: Update requirements and model per user input.\n\n- Issue type: requirement ambiguity (now addressed)\n- Issue: Timing and atomicity of emergency privilege revocation (R4.1).\n- Rationale: User clarified revocation must occur prior to first Normal mode state.\n- Action: Update requirements and model per user input.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: DelegationCoverage is a verification-only property.\n    - Category: keep\n    - Rationale: User explicitly approved this clarification.\n- Assumption: All Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked prior to or at the Emergency/Normal transition.\n    - Category: keep\n    - Rationale: Confirmed by user update for R4.2.\n- Assumption: Delegations made in Normal mode may persist across Emergency unless revoked/expired.\n    - Category: keep\n    - Rationale: Newly clarified in user update for R4.2.\n- Assumption: Emergency privileges must be revoked globally prior to first Normal mode state.\n    - Category: keep\n    - Rationale: Confirmed by user update for R4.1.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R8.1 (DelegationCoverage Verification)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity (resolved)\n- Evidence: User approval that DelegationCoverage is verification-only.\n- Recommended Update:  \n    \"R8.1: For verification purposes only, not as a strict system property, the DelegationCoverage predicate witnesses that at least one delegation exists during Emergency mode and at least one (distinct) delegation exists during Normal mode; cross-mode persistence is not required nor permitted for this witness. The absence of a delegation in either mode is permitted in actual system operation.\"\n- Implication: DelegationCoverage will be enforced only in verification/test runs.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (Delegation revocation and persistence)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity (resolved)\n- Evidence: User provided revised requirement text.\n- Recommended Update:  \n    \"All delegations made during Emergency mode must be revoked prior to (≤) the Emergency/Normal transition. Delegations made during Normal mode, before Emergency mode, can persist across Emergency mode, unless revoked by an admin or expired during Emergency. Delegations during Normal mode must continue until their expiry or revocation, regardless of the system's mode transition.\"\n- Implication: Model must encode asymmetric persistence/revocation rules for Emergency- and Normal-mode delegations.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.1 (Emergency privilege revocation)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity (resolved)\n- Evidence: User clarified revocation timing and atomicity.\n- Recommended Update:  \n    \"Emergency privileges must be revoked globally for all triggering administrators at the end of Emergency mode, prior to the first time step of Normal mode. No state in Normal mode may have any emergency privileges assigned or persisted.\"\n- Implication: Model must ensure atomic revocation and no privilege overlap into Normal mode.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ41_2: confirmed - User confirmed that DelegationCoverage is needed for verification purposes, not as a system constraint.\nQ43_1: confirmed - User clarified Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked at or prior to transition.\nQ43_3: confirmed - User confirmed DelegationCoverage is for witnessing distinct delegations per mode, not cross-mode persistence.\nQ44_1: confirmed - User clarified Emergency privileges must be revoked prior to first Normal mode state; no Normal mode state may have emergency privileges.\n