    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: The user clarified that only delegations initiated during Emergency mode require revocation before Normal mode; delegations initiated during Normal mode (even if they persist through Emergency) are not Emergency-mode delegations and do not require revocation. The model and requirements must be refined to explicitly distinguish these cases.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable (unsat predicate: DelegationCoverageEmergency)\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertR4_EmergencyDelegationsRevoked, assertDelegationCoverage)\n- Instances: N/A (relevant runs UNSAT or with assertion counterexamples)\n- Vacuity: None (failures are substantive, not trivial)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Hard metrics fail: persistent unsatisfiable predicate and assertion counterexamples. User's clarification reveals the current model does not correctly distinguish delegation types based on initiation mode, requiring further refinement and targeted requirement/model update.\n\n**CRITICAL CONVERGENCE CRITERIA:**\nRecommend FALSE if:\n- Any predicate shows unsatisfiability predicates\n- Requirement conflicts remain unresolved\n- Further refinement or requirement clarification needed\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. **Model Refinement (mandatory):**\n   - Update the model to explicitly track the initiation mode for each delegation. Introduce a property or signature attribute (e.g., `Delegation.initiatedIn: Mode`) to record whether a delegation was initiated in Emergency or Normal mode.\n   - Update all predicates/facts/constraints related to revocation (especially R4.2 and EmergencyModeGlobality) so that only delegations initiated in Emergency mode are required to be revoked prior to or at the Emergency/Normal transition. Delegations initiated in Normal mode, even if they persist through Emergency, must not be treated as Emergency-mode delegations for revocation purposes.\n   - Adjust assertions (e.g., DelegationCoverage, assertR4_EmergencyDelegationsRevoked) to use this new distinction.\n   - Add/adjust test scenarios to verify delegation persistence across Emergency/Normal transitions for both delegation types.\n\n2. **Diagnostic Experiments (if needed after above):**\n   - Rerun the diagnostic scenarios from the previous feedback, but ensure they differentiate between delegations by initiation mode.\n   - Verify that, after refinement, the unsat predicate and assertion failures are resolved and meaningful instances are produced.\n\n3. **Requirement Traceability:**\n   - Clearly document in both the model and requirement comments the distinction between Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegations to prevent future ambiguities.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Test 1: Modify DelegationCoverage to require only that Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegations can exist in separate executions (comment one out at a time), and check whether each is individually SAT.\n2. Test 2: Relax R4.2 in a diagnostic branch to allow Emergency-mode delegations to persist for one step into NormalMode, and check if DelegationCoverage becomes SAT.\n3. Test 3: Add a diagnostic predicate that witnesses only a Normal-mode delegation after Emergency end, then only an Emergency-mode delegation during Emergency, and compare which is UNSAT.\n4. Test 4: Increase scope (number of States/Delegations) to rule out accidental scope limitation (not likely needed).\n5. Test 5: Temporarily remove the revocation constraint in EmergencyModeGlobality and test if DelegationCoverage becomes SAT, confirming the source of the conflict.\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: ambiguity\n- Issue: Requirements and/or model do not explicitly distinguish between delegations initiated in Emergency mode and those initiated in Normal mode.\n- Rationale: This ambiguity leads to overconstraint, as delegations persisted from Normal mode through Emergency are incorrectly treated as Emergency-mode delegations for revocation, causing unsatisfiable assertions.\n- Action: Update requirements and model documentation to explicitly define \"Emergency-mode delegation\" as \"delegations initiated during Emergency mode,\" and clarify revocation rules accordingly.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: DelegationCoverage predicate is intended as a verification witness, not a system invariant, and does not require both delegation types in the same execution.\n- Category: keep\n- Rationale: User’s clarification supports this; the distinction is now clearer and should be preserved.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (Emergency-mode delegation revocation) and R8.1 (DelegationCoverage witness)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity / potential overconstraint\n- Evidence: User feedback and persistent assertion failures demonstrate that treating all active delegations at Emergency/Normal transition as Emergency-mode delegations is incorrect.\n- Recommended Update: \n  - R4.2 (proposed rewording): \"All delegations that are initiated during Emergency mode must be revoked before or at the Emergency/Normal transition (the first time step of Normal mode). Delegations initiated in Normal mode, even if active during Emergency, do not require revocation at this transition.\"\n  - R8.1 (proposed rewording): \"For verification purposes only, it is sufficient to show that (a) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Emergency mode, and (b) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Normal mode; these do not need to occur in the same execution.\"\n- Implication: This change ensures the model and requirements align with user intent and that only relevant delegations are subject to revocation constraints, resolving overconstraint and enabling positive verification results.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ50_1: confirmed - requirement now interpreted as allowing separate executions for Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegation witnesses, with correct delegation type distinction.\nQ51_2: confirmed - Emergency-mode delegation revocation applies only to delegations initiated during Emergency mode.\nQ49_2: confirmed - Requirements must explicitly distinguish delegation types by initiation mode as per user clarification.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Update both model and requirements to explicitly distinguish delegations by initiation mode (Emergency vs. Normal); ensure revocation rules only apply to Emergency-initiated delegations.\n- Revise assertions and predicates to align with this distinction and rerun verification.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- After above, ensure existential witness assertions are split per delegation initiation mode, and test scenarios cover each case.\n- Document distinction in both requirements and model comments.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Add regression/negative test cases to ensure no future conflation of delegation types.\n```",
