    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: User review identifies variable and structural redundancies (delegatedAt vs InitiatedIn; duringEmergency; time vs State) and requests model refactoring to remove these, requiring specific model changes and corresponding adjustments to requirements and assumptions.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertR4_EmergencyDelegationsRevoked, assertDelegationCoverage)\n- Instances: N/A (no positive instance for DelegationCoverageEmergency)\n- Vacuity: None (hard UNSAT, not vacuous)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: The user has requested significant structural changes (removal of redundant variables and consolidation of timeline/state representations), which invalidates the current model structure and requires further repairs and retesting. Persistent unsatisfiability and assertion failures remain, and hard metrics do not pass.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. **Remove Redundant Variables:**\n   - Eliminate either delegatedAt or InitiatedIn from the Delegation signature; retain only one to represent the initiation step of a delegation.\n   - Remove duringEmergency from Delegation; instead, define an auxiliary predicate or use the (now unique) initiation variable in conjunction with system mode to infer whether a delegation was initiated during Emergency mode.\n\n2. **Unify Timeline Representation:**\n   - Remove either time or State from the model; select one (preferably State for clarity) to represent progression of system steps.\n   - Update all predicates, facts, and assertions to use the unified timeline variable.\n\n3. **Enforce Emergency Mode Duration:**\n   - Add a constraint ensuring that once Emergency mode is triggered, it lasts at least one time/State step before transitioning to Normal mode.\n   - Example: For any transition where Emergency mode is entered at State s, require that the mode remains Emergency at State s+1 before it can return to Normal.\n\n4. **Refactor Delegation Mode Logic:**\n   - Adjust predicates that detect delegations initiated in Emergency mode to reference only the remaining initiation variable and system mode at that State.\n   - Remove any logic that depends on redundant variables.\n\n5. **Update/Refactor all related assertions and test scenarios:**\n   - Ensure all references to removed variables are updated.\n   - Rerun baseline and assertion tests after refactoring.\n\n6. **Document all variable removals and logic changes in the model comments for traceability.**\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Create and run two separate predicates: one requiring only Emergency-mode delegation coverage, and one only Normal-mode delegation coverage, each run independently, to confirm both are possible in separate executions.\n2. Refactor assertDelegationCoverage to require only that at least one of DelegationCoverageEmergency or DelegationCoverageNormal is satisfied, and test satisfiability.\n3. Temporarily relax the Emergency-mode delegation revocation constraint to allow Emergency-mode delegations to persist into NormalMode, and test if delegation coverage in the same execution becomes satisfiable (diagnoses whether revocation is the blocker).\n4. Create a minimal run with only EmergencyMode and delegation logic (no revocation), to confirm the minimal scenario and further isolate the blocking constraint.\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: requirement ambiguity / redundancy\n- Issue: The requirements and model definitions tolerate redundant variables (delegatedAt vs InitiatedIn; duringEmergency), leading to inconsistent or ambiguous delegation initiation logic.\n- Rationale: Redundant and overlapping variables cause maintenance and verification challenges, and may conceal or introduce subtle bugs.\n- Action: Update the requirements to refer to only a single initiation variable for delegations, and clarify how Emergency-mode initiation is determined. Remove references to redundant variables.\n\n- Issue type: missing constraint\n- Issue: The requirements do not specify a minimum Emergency mode duration.\n- Rationale: Without a minimum Emergency mode step, the system could immediately exit Emergency upon entry, violating the intended behavior.\n- Action: Add a requirement that Emergency mode, once triggered, must persist for at least one time/State step.\n\n- Issue type: timeline redundancy\n- Issue: Both time and State are used to represent system progression, leading to confusion and potential errors.\n- Rationale: Having two constructs for the same concept is unnecessary and can cause inconsistencies.\n- Action: Update requirements to specify use of only one timeline/state variable.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: R8.1 requires existential (across executions) coverage, not conjunctive (in the same execution) coverage.\n- Category: keep\n- Rationale: User has not overridden this; previous Q&A confirms.\n\n- Assumption: Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked before or at the first NormalMode state after Emergency ends.\n- Category: keep\n- Rationale: Confirmed in Q&A and requirement text.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Delegation initiation variables (delegatedAt, InitiatedIn, duringEmergency)\n- Issue Type: redundancy / ambiguity\n- Evidence: User review and persistent modeling inconsistencies; model logic depends on overlapping variables.\n- Recommended Update: Specify in requirements that only one variable (either delegatedAt or InitiatedIn) is used to mark delegation initiation; remove duringEmergency and clarify that Emergency-mode initiation is determined by the system mode at the initiation step.\n- Implication: Simplifies model and improves maintainability/traceability.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Emergency mode duration\n- Issue Type: missing constraint\n- Evidence: User review requests explicit constraint for Emergency mode to last at least one time/State step.\n- Recommended Update: Add explicit requirement: \"Emergency mode, once triggered, must persist for at least one time/State step before transition back to Normal mode is allowed.\"\n- Implication: Ensures system behavior is well-defined and prevents immediate mode reversion.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Timeline representation\n- Issue Type: redundancy / ambiguity\n- Evidence: User review; model currently uses both time and State.\n- Recommended Update: Specify in requirements/modeling guidelines that only one variable (time or State) is to be used to represent system progression.\n- Implication: Reduces confusion and risk of inconsistent step handling.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R8.1 (Delegation Coverage Verification)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity, inconsistency\n- Evidence: Persistent unsatisfiability and assertion failures for DelegationCoverageEmergency and assertDelegationCoverage; UNSAT Matrix and regression log show that enforcing both Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegation coverage in the same execution is blocked by R4.2, and that existential coverage across executions is satisfiable and intended per prior user clarifications.\n- Recommended Update: Replace R8.1 with: \"For verification purposes only, it is sufficient to show that (a) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Emergency mode, and (b) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Normal mode; these do not need to occur in the same execution. The absence of a delegation in either mode is permitted in actual system operation.\"\n- Implication: This update resolves the contradiction with R4.2, permits the model to witness Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegation coverage in separate executions, eliminates the need to enforce both properties in a single trace, and brings the assertion and predicate structure into alignment with user-confirmed intent.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\n1. Please confirm whether you prefer to retain \"delegatedAt\" or \"InitiatedIn\" as the unique variable for delegation initiation, and whether all logic should be updated to use only this variable.\n2. Should \"State\" or \"time\" be retained as the unique timeline variable for system progression? Please specify which to use.\n3. Is it acceptable for the model to infer \"duringEmergency\" for a delegation by checking the system mode at the chosen initiation variable, with no separate boolean field?\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ50_1: confirmed - User confirmed that R8.1 coverage may be existential across executions.\nQ51_2: confirmed - User confirmed that Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked before entering Normal mode, and that Normal-mode delegations may persist.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Remove redundant variables (delegatedAt/InitiatedIn, duringEmergency) and update all model logic to use only the remaining variable.\n- Consolidate time and State into a single timeline/state variable throughout requirements and model.\n- Add explicit requirement that Emergency mode must last at least one time/State step once triggered.\n\nPriority 2 (Important improvements):\n- Update R8.1 to clarify existential (not conjunctive) coverage semantics as specified above.\n- Revise and document all affected predicates, assertions, and test cases to match the updated structure and requirements.\n\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements):\n- Add model comments documenting the rationale for variable consolidation.\n- Include vacuity and negative test scenarios to confirm that no unintended overlaps or mutual coverage are required after refactoring.\n```",
