    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:  \nThe error message \"fun this/nthNext cannot call itself recursively!\" indicates that Alloy 6 does not support recursive user-defined functions. In your code, the function nthNext is defined recursively: it calls itself within its own body to compute the n-th successor in the ordering. This form of recursion is explicitly disallowed in Alloy 6. Prior failed attempts focused on function application syntax, but the actual root cause is the use of recursion in function definition, which cannot be fixed by changing brackets or call style.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:  \nReplace the recursive function nthNext with a non-recursive equivalent that computes the n-th successor using Alloy 6's built-in ordering and closure operators.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:  \n- Step 1: Delete the entire recursive definition of nthNext.\n- Step 2: Replace it with a non-recursive function using Alloy's transitive closure and set comprehensions. For example:\n  ```alloy\n  fun nthNext[s: State, n: Int]: lone State {\n    SO/next.^n[s]\n  }\n  ```\n  However, since Alloy does not support exponentiation or repeated relational join directly, use a set comprehension to define nthNext as:\n  ```alloy\n  fun nthNext[s: State, n: Int]: lone State {\n    { t: State | #(SO/prevs[s] & SO/nexts[t]) = n }\n  }\n  ```\n  Or, if you want the n-th next state from s along SO/next, use the built-in ordering module's functions if available, or define:\n  ```alloy\n  fun nthNext[s: State, n: Int]: lone State {\n    SO/nexts[s] & {t: State | #(SO/prevs[t] & SO/nexts[s]) = n}\n  }\n  ```\n  If set comprehensions are not expressive enough, consider defining a helper relation or using predicates.\n\n- Step 3: Update all calls to nthNext to match the new definition if necessary.\n\n[RATIONALE]:  \nThis fix eliminates recursion entirely, making the function Alloy 6 compliant. It is fundamentally different from altering function application syntax, directly addressing the root cause.\n\n[LESSON]:  \nRecursive user-defined functions are not allowed in Alloy 6; always use set comprehensions, built-in ordering, or relational join/closure to express iterative concepts.",
