    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: refine/narrow fix\nRationale: User clarified that both a Normal-mode delegation with expiration and an Emergency-mode delegation with timely termination/expiration must be witnessed in the model for verification, but not required in every execution. Requirement text and model must be updated for precise existential coverage.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertDelegationCoverage, DelegationCoverageEmergency UNSAT)\n- Instances: Suspicious (no Emergency-mode delegation instances present)\n- Vacuity: None\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Persistent assertion failures (UNSAT for DelegationCoverageEmergency and assertDelegationCoverage) and suspicious absence of Emergency-mode delegation instances indicate continued requirement-model misalignment. Both required scenarios (Normal-mode and Emergency-mode delegations) must be explicitly covered in at least one instance for convergence.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. Update R4.2 and R8.1 as follows in the requirements document:\n   - R4.2: \"If any delegations are initiated during Emergency mode, each must be revoked or expired before or at the Emergency/Normal transition (the first time step of Normal mode). Emergency-mode delegations are not mandatory; revocation/expiration is only required if such delegations are made.\"\n   - R8.1: \"For verification purposes, the system must show at least one execution where (a) a delegation is initiated during Normal mode and expires at its expiration time, and (b) a delegation is initiated during Emergency mode and is revoked or expired before the system reverts to Normal mode. These scenarios are not required in every execution but must be possible.\"\n2. Regenerate the EmergencyModeGlobality fact, DelegationCoverageEmergency predicate, and related assertions to ensure they require existence of both specified scenarios in some execution (not all).\n3. Review and, if necessary, adjust the model so that it supports both:\n   - At least one instance with a Normal-mode delegation that expires as specified.\n   - At least one instance with an Emergency-mode delegation that is revoked or expires before the Emergency/Normal transition.\n4. Validate that both scenarios are realized in generated instances and that assertions pass with these requirement updates.\n5. Do NOT make further model changes unrelated to Emergency-mode/Normal-mode delegation coverage until these requirements are verified.\n6. Optionally, temporarily relax Emergency-mode delegation revocation constraints to diagnose model flexibility if issues persist.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Relax the revocation constraint for Emergency-mode delegations to allow revocation at any time during EmergencyMode, not strictly at transition, and see if DelegationCoverageEmergency becomes SAT.\n2. Increase the scope (number of State and Delegation atoms) to test if scenario becomes possible.\n3. Separate the existence of Emergency-mode delegation from the revocation requirement: create a diagnostic predicate that only requires a delegation initiated in EmergencyMode, without requiring its revocation timing.\n4. Test a minimal scenario: explicitly construct a model with only one EmergencyTrigger and one Delegation initiated during EmergencyMode, and check if such a delegation can be active in any EmergencyMode state.\n5. Temporarily remove the revocation-at-transition requirement and see if the predicate becomes satisfiable; if so, the revocation logic is the blocking factor.\n```\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: ambiguity\n- Issue: Original requirements did not specify whether both delegation scenarios must be witnessed or must occur in every execution, leading to persistent unsatisfiability and scenario gaps.\n- Rationale: Model could not satisfy both existential and revocation constraints simultaneously without clarified requirement text.\n- Action: Update R4.2 and R8.1 to explicitly require both scenarios to exist in some execution, not all.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: Emergency-mode delegations must be revoked or expired before or at the Emergency/Normal transition if they exist.\n  - Category: keep (now clarified and aligned with user review)\n  - Rationale: User review affirms the revocation/expiration requirement applies only if such delegations are present.\n- Assumption: The model must show both a Normal-mode and Emergency-mode delegation scenario (each in some execution), not necessarily in every execution.\n  - Category: keep\n  - Rationale: User explicitly requires both witness scenarios for verification.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (Emergency-mode delegation revocation timing), R8.1 (Delegation Coverage Verification)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity\n- Evidence: UNSAT Matrix and assertion failures; user review confirms the existential intent and need for both scenarios.\n- Recommended Update:\n  * R4.2: \"If any delegations are initiated during Emergency mode, each must be revoked or expired before or at the Emergency/Normal transition (the first time step of Normal mode). Emergency-mode delegations are not mandatory; revocation/expiration is only required if such delegations are made.\"\n  * R8.1: \"For verification purposes, the system must show at least one execution where (a) a delegation is initiated during Normal mode and expires at its expiration time, and (b) a delegation is initiated during Emergency mode and is revoked or expired before the system reverts to Normal mode. These scenarios are not required in every execution but must be possible.\"\n- Implication: Model and verification must be updated to ensure both scenarios are possible and instantiated, but neither is required in every execution. Assertion predicates must reflect existential (not universal) coverage.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ56_1: confirmed - updated per user’s clarification.\nQ56_2: confirmed - user review aligns with prior understanding.\nQ51_1: confirmed - interpretations affirmed by user response.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Update requirements R4.2 and R8.1 per above to mandate existential witness of both delegation scenarios.\n- Refactor model predicates and facts so that both required scenarios are realized in at least one instance.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements):\n- Ensure assertion predicates for delegation coverage are existential (some execution), not universal (every execution).\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements):\n- Optionally, run diagnostic experiments with relaxed revocation constraints to confirm correct witness generation.\n```",
