    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: keep fix\nRationale: User has confirmed that for verification, existential coverage of delegations in both Emergency and Normal modes is sufficient; requirement update is needed to align with this clarified intent.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertDelegationCoverage, DelegationCoverageEmergency, DelegationCoverage UNSAT)\n- Instances: Meaningful for baseline; coverage assertions fail\n- Vacuity: Possible—coverage assertion is vacuously UNSAT due to model allowing idle (no delegation) traces\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Hard metrics fail—DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage predicates are UNSAT and assertDelegationCoverage yields counterexamples; requirement clarification and update are still needed to resolve these persistent failures.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. Do not change the model structure or further tweak predicates/facts for DelegationCoverageEmergency, DelegationCoverage, or EmergencyModeGlobality until requirements are clarified and updated per the proposed solution below.\n2. As a diagnostic experiment (per UNSAT Matrix & diagnostic recommendations):\n   - Temporarily add: `fact AtLeastOneDelegation { some Delegation }` and rerun DelegationCoverage/DelegationCoverageEmergency assertions. Observe whether these predicates become SAT, confirming the issue is due to the possibility of idle executions.\n   - Separately, run DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverageNormal as independent existential predicates using Alloy's `run` command, not `check`, to confirm that existential witnesses exist for both Emergency and Normal mode delegations.\n   - If existential coverage is confirmed as the intended requirement, replace the assertion with a `run` on DelegationCoverage and remove assertDelegationCoverage from the assertion set.\n   - Remove or reclassify assertDelegationCoverage as a coverage witness (existential check) rather than a safety invariant if the requirement update is accepted.\n3. Do not attempt to enforce delegation coverage as a system invariant unless the requirements mandate it.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Add a minimal fact: fact AtLeastOneDelegation { some Delegation } and rerun the coverage assertions—see if DelegationCoverage/DelegationCoverageEmergency become SAT.\n2. Separate the DelegationCoverage assertion into two: one for Emergency-mode delegation, one for Normal-mode delegation, and run them independently.\n3. Run the model with a run (existential) rather than check (universal) on DelegationCoverage to see if any execution can satisfy the predicate.\n4. Temporarily weaken the assertion to require only one type of delegation (Emergency or Normal) to determine if the issue is with both or either.\n5. If existential coverage is desired, replace the assertion with a run command and confirm that at least one instance exists.\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: requirement ambiguity\n- Issue: R8.1 (Delegation Coverage Verification) does not specify whether executions with no delegations are permissible, or whether each execution must include both Emergency-mode and Normal-mode delegations, leading to conflicting interpretations.\n- Rationale: Persistent unsatisfiability of DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage predicates and assertion counterexamples show that current requirements and model allow traces with no delegations, but the assertion expects at least one delegation per mode. The UNSAT Matrix and systematic analysis confirm this ambiguity is at the requirement level, not the model encoding.\n- Action: Update R8.1 to explicitly state that, for verification purposes, it is sufficient to demonstrate an instance where a delegation initiated during Emergency mode is possible, and an instance where a delegation initiated during Normal mode is possible. Clarify that executions with no delegations are permitted.\n\n- Issue type: requirement gap\n- Issue: No explicit requirement stating whether Emergency-mode delegations are required to exist, or are simply permitted; current model and requirements allow for the possibility of no delegations ever occurring.\n- Rationale: The model is satisfiable with no delegations present at all, which is acceptable for baseline requirements but contradicts the intent of coverage verification. This gap prevents meaningful coverage verification.\n- Action: Add explicit text to clarify that Emergency-mode delegations are permitted but not required, and that the absence of delegations in a given execution is acceptable for system behavior and verification.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: The system may permit idle executions (runs with no delegations at all)\n- Category: need confirmation\n- Rationale: This is not explicitly stated in the current requirements; confirmation is needed to determine whether existential or universal delegation coverage is required.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R8.1 (Delegation Coverage Verification)\n- Issue Type: ambiguity\n- Evidence: UNSAT Matrix \"recommended_follow_up\" requesting requirement clarification, repeated assertion counterexamples for assertDelegationCoverage/DelegationCoverageEmergency across multiple iterations, and deterministic diagnosis showing model allows idle traces while assertion expects delegation coverage.\n- Recommended Update: Replace R8.1 with:  \n  \"For verification purposes, the model must demonstrate an instance where a delegation initiated during Emergency mode is possible, and an instance where a delegation initiated during Normal mode is possible. These instances do not need to occur within the same execution, and executions with no delegations are permitted.\"\n- Implication: This update clarifies that coverage predicates are existential across executions, permits the model to allow executions with no delegations, and aligns verification with the clarified system flexibility.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: Emergency-mode delegation existence (implicit in R4.2 and R8.1)\n- Issue Type: requirement gap\n- Evidence: Model and requirements allow executions with no Emergency-mode delegations; coverage assertions expect at least one such delegation for verification.\n- Recommended Update: Add: \"Emergency-mode delegations are permitted but not required; the absence of such delegations in a given execution is an acceptable system behavior for verification purposes.\"\n- Implication: Removes the expectation that Emergency-mode delegations must always exist, ensuring that coverage predicates are used only for verification witness purposes and not as system invariants.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\n[Q58_2]: confirmed - User has clarified and confirmed existential coverage intent; requirement text update remains to be completed.\n[Q59_1]: confirmed - User has confirmed permissibility of idle traces; requirement text update remains to be completed.\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Update R8.1 to resolve ambiguity regarding delegation coverage by incorporating the clarified existential coverage language.\n- Add explicit requirement stating Emergency-mode delegations are permitted but not required; idle executions are acceptable.\n\nPriority 2 (Important improvements):\n- Remove or reclassify assertDelegationCoverage as a coverage witness (existential check) rather than a system invariant assertion, once requirement update is confirmed.\n- Run diagnostic experiments as specified in REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS to confirm model alignment once requirements are clarified.\n\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements):\n- Review other requirement statements for similar existential