    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: keep fix\nRationale: User confirmation clarifies R8 as an existential requirement with explicit structural preconditions (Emergency mode must occur once, each user assigned at least one role); model and predicates must be updated accordingly, and requirement ambiguity is now resolved.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Satisfiable\n- Assertions: Pass\n- Instances: N/A (DelegationCoverageEmergency/DelegationCoverage remain UNSAT)\n- Vacuity: Possible (coverage predicates never witnessed)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Hard metrics fail due to persistent unsatisfiable existential coverage predicates; model does not yet guarantee required existential scenarios under clarified requirements.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n1. Update the Alloy model to ensure:\n   - The system always enters Emergency mode exactly once during any execution (e.g., by enforcing the occurrence of EmergencyTrigger).\n   - Each user is assigned at least one role at all times or at initialization, as per clarified structural precondition.\n2. Explicitly encode these structural preconditions in the model signature/init constraints:\n   - Add a fact or initialization block ensuring every User has at least one Role.\n   - Add a fact ensuring EmergencyTrigger occurs exactly once (e.g., by constraining the trace or event sequence).\n3. Revise the DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage predicates to reflect the now unambiguous existential requirement, and ensure run commands align with these updated constraints.\n4. After implementing above, rerun all relevant predicates and assertions to confirm satisfiability and presence of required witness instances.\n5. If any coverage predicate remains UNSAT, perform targeted diagnostic runs with minimal configurations (e.g., two users, two roles, one administrator) to isolate blocking conditions.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n1. Test relaxation: Remove or weaken the existential quantification in DelegationCoverageEmergency (e.g., require only that Emergency-mode delegations are permitted, not required) and rerun to see if the predicate becomes SAT. This directly tests whether the requirement is too strong.\n2. Test separation: Create a diagnostic predicate that requires only the existence of an EmergencyTrigger event (without any delegation), to check if EmergencyMode is ever entered in any model instance.\n3. Test scope: Increase the number of Users, Roles, and Delegations in the run command to see if a larger scope enables Emergency-mode delegation.\n4. Test minimal scenario: Construct a specific scenario in which an administrator has a direct role assignment, is present during EmergencyMode, and can delegate to another user, to see if the model admits this execution.\n5. If required, relax the direct assignment constraint for delegation initiation to test whether it is the source of the conflict (not recommended unless all else fails).\n\n```\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: requirement gap (previous ambiguity now resolved)\n- Issue: Prior ambiguity in R8 regarding existential vs. permissibility coverage is now resolved with user confirmation; remaining issue is ensuring model encodes new explicit structural preconditions.\n- Rationale: The clarified requirement mandates structural conditions (Emergency mode occurrence, per-user role assignment) not previously explicit.\n- Action: Update requirements and model constraints to encode: (1) Emergency mode must occur once; (2) each user is assigned at least one role.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: R8 requires the model to guarantee that there exists at least one execution in which a delegation is initiated during Emergency mode and is active during Emergency mode, and that the system enters Emergency mode once, with each user assigned at least one role.\n- Category: keep\n- Rationale: User has now explicitly confirmed these as existential requirements.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R8: Delegation Coverage Verification\n- Issue Type: ambiguity (resolved), new structural precondition (added)\n- Evidence: User confirmation specifying existential requirement, Emergency mode occurrence, and per-user role assignment.\n- Recommended Update: Replace R8 with:\n  \"For verification purposes, the model must guarantee that there exists at least one execution in which a delegation is initiated during Emergency mode and is active during Emergency mode, and at least one execution in which a delegation is initiated during Normal mode and is active during Normal mode. The model must also guarantee that the system enters Emergency mode once and that each user is assigned at least one role. These are existential requirements; executions with no delegations are still permitted in the system.\"\n- Implication: Alloy model and predicates must enforce EmergencyTrigger occurrence, per-user role assignment, and existential coverage for both Emergency and Normal modes; verification must confirm the existence of such scenarios in all executions within scope.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\nNone\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ60_1: confirmed - User has now provided explicit existential and structural clarifications.\nQ60_2: confirmed - Now fully specified as existential.\nQ57_2: confirmed - Now fully specified as existential.\nNone - no status changes\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Update Alloy model to guarantee Emergency mode occurs once and all users are assigned at least one role.\n- Revise DelegationCoverageEmergency and DelegationCoverage predicates to reflect existential requirement.\n- Rerun verification to ensure existential scenarios are witnessed and predicates are satisfiable.\nPriority 2 (Important improvements