    "evaluator_feedback": "[DIAGNOSIS]:\nThe syntax error occurs at line 183, column 5, at the start of all et: EmergencyTrigger | et.at.mode = NormalMode. In Alloy 6, within a fact (or any block), sequential quantifier statements (e.g., all, some, one) must be separated by semicolons, not simply newlines. In this block, the quantifier on line 180 (one et: EmergencyTrigger) is directly followed by another quantifier on line 183 (all et: EmergencyTrigger) without a separating semicolon or brace, which is not valid Alloy 6 syntax. The parser expects either a comma, left brace, or vertical bar at this point, but finds \"all\" instead, causing a syntax error.\n\n[FIX INTENT]:\nInsert a semicolon to properly terminate the previous Alloy statement before beginning a new quantifier statement.\n\n[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:\n- Step 1: At the end of line 181 (after one et: EmergencyTrigger), insert a semicolon ; so it reads one et: EmergencyTrigger;.\n- Step 2: Confirm that each quantifier or statement in the fact is properly terminated with a semicolon, especially before another quantifier or statement begins.\n\n[RATIONALE]:\nThis fix follows Alloy 6's requirement for explicit statement termination within facts. Inserting the semicolon ensures the parser can correctly distinguish between separate quantifier statements, resolving the syntax error without altering the intended logic. This approach is distinct from failed attempts as it addresses the lack of statement termination, not bracketing or restructuring.\n\n[LESSON]:\nAlways terminate each quantifier or major statement in an Alloy fact/predicate with a semicolon before starting a new statement or quantifier, especially when writing multi-line or sequential constraints.",
