    "evaluator_feedback": "```\n=== NEXT ACTION DECISION ===\nDecision: keep fix\nRationale: The user's clarification resolves the EmergencyTrigger cardinality ambiguity. Model and requirements should now be aligned to enforce exactly one EmergencyTrigger per execution.\n\n=== VERIFICATION STATUS ===\n- Syntax: OK\n- Baseline: Satisfiable\n- Combined Model: Unsatisfiable\n- Assertions: Counterexamples (assertR1_OneEmergencyPerSystem, assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs)\n- Instances: Suspicious (witness predicates are SAT but core invariants are violated)\n- Vacuity: Possible (positive coverage SAT, but global safety/liveness not enforced)\n\n=== CONVERGENCE_RECOMMENDATION ===\nStatus: FALSE\nReasoning: Hard metrics fail due to persistent UNSAT predicates and assertion counterexamples. Resolution of EmergencyTrigger cardinality helps, but further model and requirement alignment is necessary before convergence.\n\n=== REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS ===\n- Update the model to require exactly one EmergencyTrigger per execution:\n    - Modify the EmergencyTriggerOccursOnce fact to enforce \"one and only one\" EmergencyTrigger in every execution.\n    - Example: fact EmergencyTriggerOccursOnce { one EmergencyTrigger }\n- Rerun verification to determine if this resolves assertR1_OneEmergencyPerSystem and related assertion failures.\n- Re-test Emergency privilege assignment constraints:\n    - Ensure the model prohibits emergencyAdmins in Normal mode and restricts emergency privileges to the two triggering admins during Emergency mode.\n- Implement the following diagnostic experiments to isolate remaining issues:\n    1. Run a minimal model fragment with only EmergencyTrigger, mode transitions, and privilege assignment, and check for SAT/UNSAT.\n    2. Temporarily relax the revocation timing constraint for Emergency-mode delegations to see if this enables satisfiability.\n    3. Increase the number of States and Users by one or two and rerun All_Requirements.\n\n\n\n--- Diagnostic Experiments (verbatim from InterpretResults, auto-included) ---\n- Run a version of the model with EmergencyTrigger cardinality relaxed (allowing zero or more triggers) and see if privilege assignment and delegation requirements are now SAT.\n- Enforce only the Emergency privilege assignment facts, with zero or more EmergencyTriggers, to test privilege scoping in isolation.\n- Run a minimal model with only EmergencyTrigger, mode transitions, and privilege assignment, and check for SAT/UNSAT.\n- Temporarily relax the revocation timing constraint for Emergency-mode delegations to see if this enables satisfiability.\n- Increase the number of States and Users by one or two and rerun the All_Requirements predicate.\n=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===\n- Issue type: ambiguity\n  - Issue: The temporal and exclusivity scope of emergency privilege assignment (emergencyAdmins) is unclear—specifically, whether emergency privileges must be strictly limited to the triggering admins only during Emergency mode, and strictly forbidden in all Normal mode states.\n  - Rationale: Counterexamples show privilege leakage into Normal mode, indicating either a missing or insufficiently specified requirement.\n  - Action: Update requirements to state explicitly that emergency privileges must never be assigned in Normal mode, and only the triggering admins may hold them during Emergency mode.\n\n- Issue type: possible overconstraint\n  - Issue: The requirement that all Emergency-mode delegations be revoked before or at the Emergency/Normal transition may be impossible to meet if revocation cannot be synchronized with the mode transition.\n  - Rationale: Persistent UNSAT for combined requirements predicates, even after several model repairs, suggests either the requirement is too strong or sequencing is unclear.\n  - Action: Clarify whether immediate revocation at transition is mandatory and feasible, or if some bounded latency is permitted.\n\n=== ASSUMPTIONS REVIEW ===\n- Assumption: EmergencyTrigger is optional (zero or one per execution).\n  - Category: reduce\n  - Rationale: User has clarified that every execution must include exactly one EmergencyTrigger; model must enforce \"one and only one.\"\n- Assumption: Emergency privileges are strictly forbidden in Normal mode.\n  - Category: keep\n  - Rationale: Prior Q&A confirm this as a strict requirement.\n- Assumption: Emergency-mode delegations must all be revoked before or at the first NormalMode state.\n  - Category: keep (pending feasibility confirmation)\n  - Rationale: User has approved this requirement, though feasibility may require further clarification.\n\n=== REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R1, R8.2, and related EmergencyTrigger cardinality statements.\n- Issue Type: ambiguity\n- Evidence: Persistent assertion failure for assertR1_OneEmergencyPerSystem, plus UNSAT Matrix and diagnostic experiments showing previous conflict between existential and universal EmergencyTrigger requirements.\n- Recommended Update: Explicitly state that every execution must contain exactly one EmergencyTrigger, causing the system to enter Emergency mode.\n- Implication: The model and assertions can now globally require and enforce the presence of one and only one EmergencyTrigger in every execution.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R3.3, R4.1 (emergency privilege scoping).\n- Issue Type: ambiguity\n- Evidence: Counterexample to assertR3_OnlyTriggeringAdminsGetEmergencyPrivs; UNSAT Matrix analysis points to privilege leakage and lack of sufficient constraint.\n- Recommended Update: State explicitly that emergency privileges are always empty in Normal mode, and only the two triggering administrators may ever hold them, only during Emergency mode.\n- Implication: Narrows permissible behaviors; clarifies forbidden privilege leakage; enables strict global invariants for privilege assignment.\n\n- Affected Requirement/Assumption/Constraint: R4.2 (Emergency delegation revocation timing).\n- Issue Type: possible overconstraint/ambiguity\n- Evidence: Persistent UNSAT and diagnostic experiment proposals to relax revocation timing.\n- Recommended Update: Clarify if revocation must be immediate at the Emergency/Normal transition, or if a small bounded delay is allowed, and what, if any, exceptions exist.\n- Implication: Modifies what is required at the mode transition and may make the combined requirements satisfiable.\n\n=== UPDATED USER QUESTIONS ===\n1. Is it strictly required that emergency privileges (emergencyAdmins) are always empty in Normal mode, and that only the two triggering administrators may hold these privileges during Emergency mode—never any others, and never in Normal mode?\n2. For delegations initiated during Emergency mode, must all such delegations be revoked exactly at the Emergency/Normal transition (i.e., before the first NormalMode state), or is it acceptable for revocation to occur with a bounded delay after the transition?\n\n=== Q&A STATUS UPDATE ===\nQ66_1: confirmed – updated to require exactly one EmergencyTrigger per execution, per user clarification\nQ50_3: confirmed – emergency privileges forbidden for non-triggering admins at all times\nQ51_3: confirmed – immediate revocation of Emergency-mode delegations at transition required\n\n=== HANDOFF TO RE ===\nPriority 1 (Critical blockers): \n- Explicit scoping and temporal exclusivity of emergency privilege assignment (Question 1 above; R3.3/R4.1 update).\nPriority 2 (Important improvements): \n- Clarification on Emergency-mode delegation revocation timing and feasibility (Question 2 above; R4.2 update).\n- Align model facts/predicates with clarified requirements after above answers.\n- Implement and analyze diagnostic experiments to isolate model/requirement conflicts.\nPriority 3 (Optional enhancements): \n- Review and update minimum user and clearance requirements for delegation feasibility.\n- Consider formalizing existential vs. universal coverage requirements for other behaviors (delegation, Emergency mode entry) for future verification.\n```",
